pb0:{tag:'Thesis · Master',title:'A Study of Macao Funerary Architectural Imagery',
 meta:[['Type',"Master's thesis"],['Institution','Faculty of Humanities and Arts, Macau University of Science and Technology'],['Degree','Master of Fine Arts · History of Fine Arts'],['Completed','Defended June 2024'],['English title','A Study on the Architectural Images of Tombstones in Macao'],['Scope','The 17 cemeteries extant in Macao'],['Indexed in','MUST Library institutional thesis repository']],
 body:`<div class="sub">Abstract (as recorded in the MUST Library thesis repository)</div><p>The tomb architecture in Macao is a typical representative of the fusion of Chinese and Western cultures. Each cemetery in Macau has its own characteristics, and each cemetery is closely related to the development of the city of Macau. However, due to the taboo of cemeteries, the religious, cultural, artistic and architectural values contained in the tombs have been little known. The types and images of the tomb buildings in Macao are diverse, rich in content, and have profound cultural deposits. Due to the influence of political, economic, cultural and religious factors, the architectural images of tombs have been given a unique form of artistic expression. Therefore, an in-depth study of the status quo of tomb architecture in Macao and an analysis of the content, application, cultural system and significance of tomb architecture images are of great significance to deeply explain the concept expression behind the images, enrich the research of Macao culture and art and the history of Macao art, and inherit and protect the cultural heritage of Macao.</p>
 <div class="sub">Method and scope</div><ul>
 <li>Architectural ethnography, documentary research, iconography and iconology, and interdisciplinary research</li>
 <li>A comprehensive survey of the tombstone architecture of the 17 cemeteries extant in Macao — structure and form, and the identification, meaning and application of their imagery</li>
 <li>Field investigation to establish the rules governing the use of images, clarify their correspondences, interpret their meaning, and compare tombstones across cultural backgrounds</li></ul>
 <div class="sub">Main conclusions</div><ul>
 <li>A stylised correspondence exists between the content of tombstone imagery and architectural structure</li>
 <li>Through the symbolism of structure and image, tombstone architecture creates a spiritual space that answers the spiritual needs of Macao's people; that space arises from the configuration and combination of structure and image</li>
 <li>Through the interrelation of structure and image, religious figures are made ordinary and nature worship secularised, providing a cultural background for ordinary people to ascend to heaven</li>
 <li>Deeply shaped by foreign cultures and religions and fused with Chinese tradition, tombstone imagery has produced a distinctive Macao phenomenon of Chinese–Western artistic fusion</li></ul>
 <div class="sub">Structure</div><ul>
 <li>Ch.1 Introduction: definition of concepts; motivation and significance; state of research; method; principal innovations</li>
 <li>Ch.2 The cemeteries and tombstone architecture of Macao (environment, overview, present condition)</li>
 <li>Ch.3 Tombstone imagery: types, application, forms of expression</li>
 <li>Ch.4 Interpretation of meaning: natural meaning, conventional meaning, symbolic meaning</li>
 <li>Ch.5 Comparative analysis: local Chinese–Western differences; compared with mainland Chinese tradition; compared with Western Christian tombs</li>
 <li>Ch.6 Conclusion | References | Appendix: fieldwork manuscripts</li></ul>
 <a class="dl" href="https://must.primo.exlibrisgroup.com/permalink/853MUST_INST/reglfj/alma991003484771305076" target="_blank">MUST Library thesis repository · full record ↓</a>
 <p class="dim">The theme was later carried into a series of international presentations and papers: Area Studies · Beijing Forum 2026, Lingnan University Postgraduate Conference 2026, Edinburgh Student Congress 2026 (Space and Culture, forthcoming in sine), the 3rd Global South studies symposium, and a Third Prize paper at the "Guangqi Cup" national student paper competition. The library records the title as A Study on the Architectural Images of Tombstones in Macao.</p>`,
 tags:['Funerary architecture','Macao','Art history','Iconography','Architectural ethnography','Chinese–Western fusion']},

